# 10. In-Session Discomfort and Swap Relationship

This is a required end-to-end release relationship.

Trace:

```text
system input
  user reports discomfort from the active exercise card
→ giver
  pain feedback flow + substitution/adaptation policy
→ output
  easier variation, role-preserving swap, safe skip or unresolved-safe action
→ UI receiver
  pain modal, substitution choices, active exercise card and confirmation state
→ persistence effect
  session draft, exercise feedback, substitution history, future adaptation,
  personal block or retest state
```

## Required visible actions

The active workout experience must expose understandable actions equivalent to:

* Report discomfort
* Make it easier
* Swap exercise
* Skip safely
* Stop the session when appropriate
* Remove from my program / block until reset, where already supported

Do not expose raw internal labels such as:

```text
sacrifice
test
modify
```

Translate internal actions into plain user language.

Internal enums may remain unchanged where compatibility requires it.

## Swap truth requirements

Every offered swap must:

* be equipment-legal;
* respect confirmed supports and anchors;
* respect the pain signal;
* preserve the original movement purpose where possible;
* preserve true role classification;
* contain complete coaching;
* avoid personally blocked exercises;
* avoid previously rejected painful variants where policy requires;
* remain deterministic;
* and pass the quality gate.

A squat may not be replaced by a breathing drill and remain counted as a squat.

A true vertical pull may not be replaced by a pull-apart and remain classified as true vertical pulling.

A preparation exercise may not satisfy a primary strength role.

## No-valid-swap state

When no valid role-preserving swap exists:

* do not show an empty or broken picker;
* do not insert an unrelated movement;
* explain that Praxis could not find a safe equivalent in the confirmed setup;
* offer a safe skip or stop action;
* preserve the honest weekly limitation;
* save the feedback;
* and allow future programming to adapt.

## State continuity

Test the result through:

* choosing the replacement,
* returning to the active card,
* moving Next,
* moving Back,
* refreshing,
* leaving and resuming the session,
* completing the session,
* opening exercise history,
* and generating or loading the next relevant session.

The user must not see the original painful exercise silently restored during the same session.

